@startuml
skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #374151
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.3
skinparam state {
  BorderColor #1F2937
  FontColor #111827
}
skinparam note {
  BorderColor #1F2937
  FontColor #111827
}

title Bisimulation invariance: a behavioral predicate cannot distinguish bisimilar processes

' ── Two bisimilar LTS side by side: process P (left) and its bisimilar copy
' Q (right) — e.g. two copies of `a.b`. States colored amber = behavioral.
' NOTE on structure: a PlantUML STATE diagram admits only DIRECTED edges
' (`->`), so the symmetric bisimilarity relation `∼` is drawn as a single
' dashed amber arrow and labelled as the symmetric relation it denotes
' (the largest bisimulation); the spanning note hangs off a state (the
' state engine rejects free `note .. state` links and non-directional edges).
state "P0  (process P)" as P0 #FEF3C7
state "P1" as P1 #FEF3C7
state "P2" as P2 #FEF3C7

state "Q0  (process Q ∼ P)" as Q0 #FEF3C7
state "Q1" as Q1 #FEF3C7
state "Q2" as Q2 #FEF3C7

' the two LTS, each a `step`-chain running downward
P0 -down-> P1 : step
P1 -down-> P2 : step
Q0 -down-> Q1 : step
Q1 -down-> Q2 : step

' the bisimilarity witness between the two roots (dashed amber; ∼ is symmetric)
P0 -[#B45309,dashed]-> Q0 : ∼ (bisimilar, symmetric)

note bottom of P2
  `P0 ∼ Q0` ⇒ they satisfy the SAME behavioral predicates:
  `evaluate(AG ¬bad, P0) = evaluate(AG ¬bad, Q0)`  (Theorem 4.2).
  A behavioral guard is a property of behavior, not representation
  — well-defined on the quotient `S / ∼`.
  (Hennessy–Milner: bisimilar ⟺ same modal formulas.)
end note

legend right
|= Color / mark |= Meaning |
|<#FEF3C7> | behavioral state (a process in the LTS) |
| ∼ | bisimilarity — the largest bisimulation (a greatest fixed point); symmetric |
endlegend
@enduml
